{"id": "edeaa80e-aad0-42be-8c5f-d9ffa718715b", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T20:59:13.949835", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "08861669-a874-461c-bde8-019326453dd2", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-23d7b4fe-0236-4907-bb78-11dbd459c288", "tool_call": {"id": "call-23d7b4fe-0236-4907-bb78-11dbd459c288", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Propose experiment design approach for review\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Proposed experiment design approach\\n\\n### (1) Independent variables\\n- **M — microbatch count per training step.** Primary x-axis. Sweep over a range that spans both \\\"few microbatches\\\" (small M) and \\\"many microbatches\\\" (large M). Suggested set, expressed in multiples of pipeline depth P: {2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P, 64P} (and constrained to the M≥(P−1) lower bound that 1F1B-style schedules need, and to the larger lower bounds that ZB-H1 and ZB-H2 require to fill their warmup). The actual numeric values must be confirmed once the repo is available; below I treat this set as the candidate sweep and flag it as an assumption.\\n- **P — pipeline depth (number of stages).** Treated as a second independent variable, not just a control, because the claim \\\"ZB-2p maintains high throughput with fewer microbatches\\\" can only be properly evaluated at multiple depths (the warmup/window cost scales with P). Suggested set: {4, 8, 16} stages.\\n- **Method ∈ {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p}.** Categorical IV. Held at one value per run; all four are run at every (M, P) cell.\\n- *(For 1F1B-I only)* **v — number of virtual stages.** This is a sub-knob of 1F1B-I; fix v = 2 to keep the design orthogonal, or sweep v ∈ {2, 4} as a secondary axis. Flagged as assumption.\\n\\n### (2) Dependent variable — throughput, how it's measured\\n- **Primary metric:** sustained training throughput in **samples/sec** (and equivalently **tokens/sec**), computed over a fixed measurement window that excludes pipeline warmup.\\n- **Secondary metrics** (used only as sanity checks, not as the headline answer):\\n  - Bubble fraction estimated from the schedule analytically or from a profiler timeline.\\n  - Step time per training step (ms/step).\\n  - Memory peak per stage (to detect that we are not silently trading throughput for OOM).\\n- **Measurement protocol:** skip the first W_warmup steps of every run, then average over W_measure steps. Both W_warmup and W_measure are treated as fixed constants (see §3). Discard or re-run any step where the global batch does not equal B = M × b (constant b).\\n- **Per-cell replication:** ≥3 independent seeds per (method, M, P) cell, so we can report mean and a confidence interval rather than a single point. Seeds must be orthogonal to the run index.\\n\\n### (3) Controls and constants\\nTo isolate the effect of M on pipeline efficiency, the following are held fixed across all runs:\\n- **Per-microbatch size b** (samples per microbatch). This keeps the work per microbatch identical so M only changes how many microbatches are in flight.\\n  - *Consequential consequence:* total global batch size per step equals B = M · b, which therefore grows with M. I am flagging this as the explicit choice (constant b, variable B). The alternative (constant B, variable b) is also defensible and would test a different question; I recommend constant b unless you say otherwise.\\n- **Model size** (parameter count, depth within a stage, hidden dim, sequence length). I'll propose a single GPT-style transformer at a size the repo's examples support, but **the exact architecture and size are an assumption that must be confirmed against the repo.**\\n- **Precision** (e.g., bf16/fp16/fp32) and **optimizer state sharding approach** (e.g., ZeRO stage), kept identical across methods.\\n- **Hardware:** P GPUs of the same type, all-to-all interconnect same topology, NCCL version pinned. P GPUs = pipeline depth P, so we do not also vary 3D parallelism.\\n- **Warmup window W_warmup and measurement window W_measure**, both in steps, expressed as functions of P and M (e.g., W_warmup = max(50, ⌈P⌉) steps; W_measure large enough that total measurement time ≥ ~10 s wall clock — exact numbers depend on hardware speed and need confirmation).\\n- **Software stack** (commit SHA of the repo, CUDA, NCCL, PyTorch, DeepSpeed/Megatron-LM or whichever framework the repo uses) — pinned for the whole sweep.\\n\\n### (4) Planned analysis\\n- **Curves.** Throughput vs M, one curve per method, one panel per P. This is the figure that directly tests the question's claim visually: do 1F1B / 1F1B-I / ZB-1p curves rise with M, and is ZB-2p's curve relatively flat at the top of the family?\\n- **Comparison framing.** Two complementary comparisons:\\n  1. *Within-method trend.* For each method, fit throughput as a function of M (e.g., saturation-curve model like T(M) = T_∞ · (M / (M + M₀)) or a linearizable form of the bubble-fraction model). Report fitted T_∞ and M₀. A larger M₀ (or lower rise) for ZB-2p vs the others is what we are looking for.\\n  2. *Cross-method at matched M.* At each M, rank the four methods by throughput. Specifically test the headline claim: at small M (e.g., M ≈ 2P), is ZB-2p at or near the top? At large M (M ≥ 16P), do all methods converge?\\n- **Statistical handling.** Per-cell mean ± 95% CI across seeds (t-based given small n). For trend claims, also report a Spearman rank correlation between M and throughput per method and test whether ZB-2p's slope is significantly smaller than the other three's (interaction term in a linear model: throughput ∼ M · method). Flag interaction-test details for review.\\n- **Gap-to-peak diagnostic.** Plot T(M)/T(64P) — i.e., throughput at each M normalized by the throughput at the largest M for the same method and P. This makes the \\\"flatter curve for ZB-2p\\\" claim legible at a glance and is robust to absolute differences across P.\\n\\n### (5) Feasibility under the design-only constraint\\n- I cannot run the experiment: GPU is disabled, network is disabled, dependencies are not preinstalled, and the repo itself is not present in `/workspace`. The output is a design document plus the analysis plan; no measurements are produced and none are claimed.\\n- The plan above deliberately specifies `config-style` knobs (method name, M, P, v) that mirror common pipeline-parallel APIs but are written as assumptions, not as facts about this repo.\\n- Concrete feasibility risks to flag now (not invent objections):\\n  1. The repo's actual way to select a schedule is unknown (CLI flag vs. YAML key vs. Python class). Until the repo is supplied, I cannot map the four method names onto the codebase's terminology.\\n  2. Default mixed precision, optimizer sharding, sequence packing, and any all-reduce/bucket configuration must be confirmed from the repo; otherwise they become hidden variables that conflate pipeline scheduling with overhead differences.\\n  3. ZB-2p typically requires M to be at least ~3(P−1) microbatches to fill its warmup region; if the sweep's smallest M is below that floor, the ZB-2p row will not be measurable but will need to be disqualified with a documented reason rather than silently dropped.\\n\\n### Assumptions introduced (separate from any confirmed task requirements)\\n- A1. The repo exposes all four schedules as selectable options and we can run each with the same global batch / per-microbatch control described above.\\n- A2. Each schedule's \\\"method name\\\" is one of {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p} or a direct synonym (e.g., ZB-H1, ZB-H2, interleaved 1F1B).\\n- A3. Per-microbatch size b can be held fixed while M is varied (constant-b design, not constant-B).\\n- A4. Pipeline depth P equals the number of GPUs devoted to the pipeline dimension, with no data or tensor parallelism mixed in.\\n- A5. Each run is reproducible from a seed to within the measurement window's tolerance.\\n- A6. Wall-clock per measurement is long enough to make wall-clock shares of warmup negligible relative to measurement (i.e., we are measuring steady state, not startup).\\n\\n### Uncertainties (items I cannot resolve from `task.json` alone)\\n- U1. Exact method names and how they are selected in the repo's CLI/config.\\n- U2. The set of valid M values per method (lower bounds for ZB schedules); the proposed {2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P, 64P} grid may need to be adjusted to respect method-specific minimums.\\n- U3. Default precision, optimizer, and any automatic ZeRO/3D-parallel coupling the repo enables by default.\\n- U4. Whether the repo has an existing throughput benchmark I should reuse vs. needing a wrapper.\\n- U5. The minimal architecture (parameters) the user wants to run, and whether one architecture is sufficient or a small architecture-size sensitivity study is desired.\\n- U6. Whether to keep total global batch B fixed instead of per-microbatch b fixed (question-level decision, not repo-dependent).\\n\\nI have not yet written the full design. I am waiting for your review of this approach — in particular, I would value your direction on:\\n- U3 (default precision / optimizer / parallelism stacking in the repo),\\n- U4 (reusing an existing benchmark vs. a fresh harness),\\n- U5 (architecture choice),\\n- U6 (constant-b vs. constant-B framing),\\n- and whether v ∈ {2} is acceptable for 1F1B-I.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository inspection results:\\n\\n1. `ls -la /workspace` returns only `task.json` (651 bytes).\\n2. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` returns only `/workspace/task.json`.\\n3. `find / -iname \\\"*zero*bubble*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*1f1b*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*pipeline*\\\"` returns no project files — only unrelated system paths (`npm/minipass-pipeline`, `botocore/.../codepipeline`, `libpipeline1`).\\n4. `/home/actor` and `/tmp` are empty.\\n5. `/proc/cpuinfo` and `free -h` show 24 CPUs, 125 GB RAM; GPU is disabled per `task.json`; Python 3.12.3 is available.\\n6. `task.json` includes the explicit note: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n\\nTherefore no source code, README, pipeline/zero-bubble directory, configuration file, benchmark, or script was observed. The four pipeline scheduling methods (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p) are referenced in the question but are not present in the workspace. Any method-specific defaults, config keys, file paths, CLI flags, or API names in this plan are explicitly flagged as assumptions (A1–A6) and uncertainties (U1–U6), not observations.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"A6. Wall-clock per measurement is long enough to make startup shares negligible (we are measuring steady state, not warmup).</item>\",\"$text\":\"A5. Each run is reproducible from a seed to within the measurement window's tolerance.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A4. Pipeline depth P equals the number of GPUs devoted to the pipeline dimension, with no data or tensor parallelism mixed in.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A3. Per-microbatch size b is held fixed while M varies (constant-b design, not constant-B).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A2. Each schedule's \\\"method name\\\" is one of {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p} or a direct synonym (e.g., ZB-H1, ZB-H2, interleaved 1F1B).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A1. The repo exposes all four schedules as selectable options and we can run each with the same global batch / per-microbatch control described above.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"U6. Whether to keep total global batch B fixed instead of per-microbatch b fixed (question-level decision, not repo-dependent).</item>\",\"$text\":\"U5. The minimal architecture (parameters) the user wants to run, and whether one architecture is sufficient or a small architecture-size sensitivity study is desired.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U4. Whether the repo has an existing throughput benchmark that should be reused vs. needing a wrapper.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U3. Default precision, optimizer, and any automatic ZeRO/3D-parallel coupling the repo enables by default.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U2. The set of valid M values per method (lower bounds for ZB schedules); the proposed {2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P, 64P} grid may need to be adjusted to respect method-specific minimums.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U1. Exact method names and how they are selected in the repo's CLI/config.</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-44672656-c027-4332-8e33-6f389ae51973", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
