rcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Propose experiment design approach for review\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## Proposed experiment design approach\\n\\n### (1) Independent variables\\n- M \\u2014 microbatch count per training step. Primary x-axis. Sweep over a range that spans both \\\"few microbatches\\\" (small M) and \\\"many microbatches\\\" (large M). Suggested set, expressed in multiples of pipeline depth P: {2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P, 64P} (and constrained to the M \\u2265 (P-1) lower bound that 1F1B-style schedules need, and to the larger lower bounds that ZB-H1 and ZB-H2 require to fill their warmup). The actual numeric values must be confirmed once the repo is available; below I treat this set as the candidate sweep and flag it as an assumption.\\n- P \\u2014 pipeline depth (number of stages). Treated as a second independent variable, not just a control, because the claim \\\"ZB-2p maintains high throughput with fewer microbatches\\\" can only be properly evaluated at multiple depths (warmup/window cost scales with P). Suggested set: {4, 8, 16} stages.\\n- Method \\u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p}. Categorical IV. Held at one value per run; all four are run at every (M, P) cell.\\n- (For 1F1B-I only) v \\u2014 number of virtual stages. Sub-knob of 1F1B-I; fix v = 2 to keep the design orthogonal, or sweep v \\u2208 {2, 4} as a secondary axis. Flagged as assumption.\\n\\n### (2) Dependent variable \\u2014 throughput, how it's measured\\n- Primary metric: sustained training throughput in samples/sec (and equivalently tokens/sec), computed over a fixed measurement window that excludes pipeline warmup.\\n- Secondary metrics (sanity checks, not headline answer):\\n  - Bubble fraction estimated from the schedule analytically or from a profiler timeline.\\n  - Step time per training step (ms/step).\\n  - Memory peak per stage (to detect that we are not silently trading throughput for OOM).\\n- Measurement protocol: skip the first W_warmup steps of every run, then average over W_measure steps. Both W_warmup and W_measure are treated as fixed constants (see \\u00a73). Discard or re-run any step where the global batch does not equal B = M \\u00d7 b (constant b).\\n- Per-cell replication: \\u22653 independent seeds per (method, M, P) cell, so we can report mean and a confidence interval rather than a single point. Seeds must be orthogonal to the run index.\\n\\n### (3) Controls and constants\\nTo isolate the effect of M on pipeline efficiency, the following are held fixed across all runs:\\n- Per-microbatch size b (samples per microbatch). Keeps the work per microbatch identical so M only changes how many microbatches are in flight.\\n  - Consequential consequence: total global batch size per step equals B = M \\u00b7 b, which therefore grows with M. Flagged as the explicit choice (constant b, variable B). The alternative (constant B, variable b) tests a different question; I recommend constant b unless you say otherwise.\\n- Model size (parameter count, depth within a stage, hidden dim, sequence length). Single GPT-style transformer at a size the repo's examples support; exact architecture and size are an assumption that must be confirmed against the repo.\\n- Precision (e.g., bf16/fp16/fp32) and optimizer state sharding approach (e.g., ZeRO stage), kept identical across methods.\\n- Hardware: P GPUs of the same type, all-to-all interconnect same topology, NCCL version pinned. P GPUs = pipeline depth P, so we do not also vary 3D parallelism.\\n- Warmup window W_warmup and measurement window W_measure, both in steps, expressed as functions of P and M (e.g., W_warmup = max(50, P) steps; W_measure large enough that total measurement time \\u2265 ~10 s wall clock \\u2014 exact numbers depend on hardware speed and need confirmation).\\n- Software stack (commit SHA of the repo, CUDA, NCCL, PyTorch, DeepSpeed/Megatron-LM or whichever framework the repo uses) pinned for the whole sweep.\\n\\n### (4) Planned analysis\\n- Curves. Throughput vs M, one curve per method, one panel per P. This figure directly tests the question's claim visually: do 1F1B / 1F1B-I / ZB-1p curves rise with M, and is ZB-2p's curve relatively flat at the top of the family?\\n- Comparison framing. Two complementary comparisons:\\n  1. Within-method trend. For each method, fit throughput as a function of M (e.g., saturation-curve model T(M) = T_\\u221e \\u00b7 M / (M + M\\u2080) or a linearizable form of the bubble-fraction model). Report fitted T_\\u221e and M\\u2080. A larger M\\u2080 (or lower rise) for ZB-2p vs the others is what we are looking for.\\n  2. Cross-method at matched M. At each M, rank the four methods by throughput. Specifically test the headline claim: at small M (e.g., M \\u2248 2P), is ZB-2p at or near the top? At large M (M \\u2265 16P), do all methods converge?\\n- Statistical handling. Per-cell mean \\u00b1 95% CI across seeds (t-based given small n). For trend claims, also report a Spearman rank correlation between M and throughput per method and test whether ZB-2p's slope is significantly smaller than the other three's (interaction term in a linear model: throughput \\u223c M \\u00b7 method). Flag interaction-test details for review.\\n- Gap-to-peak diagnostic. Plot T(M) / T(64P) \\u2014 throughput at each M normalized by the throughput at the largest M for the same method and P. Makes the \\\"flatter curve for ZB-2p\\\" claim legible at a glance and is robust to absolute differences across P.\\n\\n### (5) Feasibility under the design-only constraint\\n- I cannot run the experiment: GPU is disabled, network is disabled, dependencies are not preinstalled, and the repo itself is not present in /workspace. The output is a design document plus the analysis plan; no measurements are produced and none are claimed.\\n- The plan specifies `config-style` knobs (method name, M, P, v) that mirror common pipeline-parallel APIs but are written as assumptions, not as facts about this repo.\\n- Concrete feasibility risks to flag now (not invent objections):\\n  1. The repo's actual way to select a schedule is unknown (CLI flag vs. YAML key vs. Python class). Until the repo is supplied, I cannot map the four method names onto the codebase's terminology.\\n  2. Default mixed precision, optimizer sharding, sequence packing, and any all-reduce/bucket configuration must be confirmed from the repo; otherwise they become hidden variables that conflate pipeline scheduling with overhead differences.\\n  3. ZB-2p typically requires M to be at least ~3(P-1) microbatches to fill its warmup region; if the sweep's smallest M is below that floor, the ZB-2p row will not be measurable and must be disqualified with a documented reason rather than silently dropped.\\n\\n### Decision points I'd like to confirm before drafting the full design\\n- Constant-b vs constant-B framing for total batch size (default proposed: constant-b).\\n- Default precision / optimizer / parallelism stacking (to be pinned once the repo is available).\\n- Reuse an existing benchmark vs a fresh harness.\\n- Architecture choice (single GPT-style transformer at a size the repo's examples support).\\n- v \\u2208 {2} for 1F1B-I, or sweep v \\u2208 {2, 4}.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository inspection results:\\n\\n1. `ls -la /workspace` returns only `task.json` (651 bytes).\\n2. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` returns only `/workspace/task.json`.\\n3. `find / -iname \\\"*zero*bubble*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*1f1b*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*pipeline*\\\"` returns no project files \\u2014 only unrelated system paths (`npm/minipass-pipeline`, `botocore/.../codepipeline`, `libpipeline1`).\\n4. `/home/actor` and `/tmp` are empty.\\n5. `/proc/cpuinfo` and `free -h` show 24 CPUs, 125 GB RAM; GPU is disabled per `task.json`; Python 3.12.3 is available.\\n6. `task.json` includes the explicit note: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n\\nTherefore no source code, README, pipeline/zero-bubble directory, configuration file, benchmark, or script was observed. The four pipeline scheduling methods (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p) are referenced in the question but are not present in the workspace. Any method-specific defaults, config keys, file paths, CLI flags, or API names in this plan are explicitly flagged as assumptions and uncertainties, not observations.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. The repo exposes all four schedules as selectable options and we can run each with the same per-microbatch and global-batch control described above.\\\\n\", \"A2. Each schedule's \\\"method name\\\" is one of {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p} or a direct synonym (e.g., ZB-H1, ZB-H2, interleaved 1F1B).\\\\n\", \"A3. Per-microbatch size b is held fixed while M varies (constant-b design, not constant-B).\\\\n\", \"A4. Pipeline depth P equals the number of GPUs devoted to the pipeline dimension, with no data or tensor parallelism mixed in.\\\\n\", \"A5. Each run is reproducible from a seed to within the measurement window's tolerance.\\\\n\", \"A6. Wall-clock per measurement is long enough to make startup shares negligible (we are measuring steady state, not warmup).\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1. Exact method names and how they are selected in the repo's CLI/config.\\\\n\", \"U2. The set of valid M values per method (lower bounds for ZB schedules); the proposed {2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P, 64P} grid may need to be adjusted to respect method-specific minimums.\\\\n\", \"U3. Default precision, optimizer, and any automatic ZeRO/3D-parallel coupling the repo enables by default.\\\\n\", \"U4. Whether the repo has an existing throughput benchmark that should be reused vs. needing a wrapper.\\\\n\", \"U5. The minimal architecture (parameters) the user wants to run, and whether one architecture is sufficient or a small architecture-size sensitivity study is desired.\\\\n\", \"U6. Whether to keep total global batch B fixed instead of per-microbatch b fixed (question-level decision, not repo-dependent).\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-c4725e71-ed6e-4d13-8fbd-aa573952e7fa", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
